var e={title:{pt:"Origens da Vida, Astrobiologia e DNA Antigo",en:"Origins of Life, Astrobiology, and Ancient DNA"},level:"S",subject:"biology",objectives:["Compreender Miller-Urey, mundo de RNA, LUCA","Reconhecer endossimbiose, Asgard, eukaryogênese","Identificar Marte/Europa/Encélado biosignatures","Avaliar Pääbo Nobel 2022, Neandertal, Denisovan"],difficulty:5,authorNotes:`Origins: Miller-Urey 1953, RNA world Gilbert 1986 (Cech/Altman Nobel 1989), Sutherland
cianosulfidico, hydrothermal Russell/Martin/Lane, Szostak protocells (Nobel 2009 telomeres
separately). Endossimbiose Margulis 1967. Asgard archaea Ettema 2015 Lokiarchaeota.
Astrobiology: Perseverance Jezero 2021, Europa Clipper launch out 2024, Enceladus plumes
Cassini, Titan Huygens. Pääbo Nobel Medicine 2022 ancient DNA Neandertal+Denisovan. JWST
exoplanet biosignatures DMS K2-18b 2023.
`,example:{question:{pt:"Pääbo Nobel Medicine 2022 reconheceu…",en:"Pääbo 2022 Nobel in Medicine recognized…"},choices:["Pioneering ancient DNA paleogenomics — sequencing of Neanderthal and Denisovan genomes revealing interbreeding with modern humans and human evolution insights","Discovery of CRISPR-Cas9 genome editing","First sequencing of the human genome project","Development of mRNA vaccines for COVID-19"],correctAnswer:"Pioneering ancient DNA paleogenomics — sequencing of Neanderthal and Denisovan genomes revealing interbreeding with modern humans and human evolution insights",rationale:{pt:'Nobel Medicine 2022 — Svante Pääbo (Max Planck Institute Evolutionary Anthropology Leipzig) "for his discoveries concerning the genomes of extinct hominins and human evolution". Trabalho seminal: ancient DNA recovery technology (decades developed), Neandertal mitochondrial genome 2008, Neandertal nuclear genome 2010 Science (Green Krause Briggs et al — ~1.3-2.6% Neandertal DNA non-African modern humans), Denisovan finger bone discovery 2010 (Pääbo lab Krause from Denisova cave Siberia), Denisovan genome 2010-2012 (~3-6% in Melanesians/Papuans), composite individual Denny 2018 Nature (Neandertal mother + Denisovan father, F1 hybrid). Pääbo founded ancient DNA field metodologically — clean room procedures contamination control, library preparation degraded short reads, computational ancient/modern admixture analysis. Subsequent: Reich Harvard scaling ~10000 ancient genomes globally, paleogenomics population history insights (Yamnaya migrations Europe, agricultural expansions, etc). Father Sune Bergström também Nobel Medicine 1982 prostaglandins — second-generation laureate.',en:'2022 Nobel in Medicine — Svante Pääbo (Max Planck Institute Evolutionary Anthropology Leipzig) "for his discoveries concerning the genomes of extinct hominins and human evolution". Seminal work: ancient DNA recovery technology (decades developed), Neanderthal mitochondrial genome 2008, Neanderthal nuclear genome 2010 Science (Green/Krause/Briggs et al — ~1.3-2.6% Neanderthal DNA in non-African modern humans), Denisovan finger bone discovery 2010 (Pääbo lab Krause from Denisova cave Siberia), Denisovan genome 2010-2012 (~3-6% in Melanesians/Papuans), composite individual Denny 2018 Nature (Neanderthal mother + Denisovan father, F1 hybrid). Pääbo methodologically founded the ancient DNA field — clean-room procedures, contamination control, library preparation for degraded short reads, computational ancient/modern admixture analysis. Subsequent: Reich Harvard scaling to ~10000 ancient genomes globally, paleogenomics population history insights (Yamnaya migrations into Europe, agricultural expansions, etc). Father Sune Bergström also Nobel Medicine 1982 for prostaglandins — second-generation laureate.'}},passCriteria:{minAccuracyPercent:85,maxAvgSecondsPerExercise:14},pages:[{title:{pt:"Página 1 — Origens e evolução",en:"Page 1 — Origins and evolution"},exercises:[{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"Miller-Urey experiment (Science 1953) demonstrated…",en:"Miller-Urey experiment (Science 1953) demonstrated…"},choices:["Spontaneous formation of amino acids and other organic precursors from simple inorganic gases (CH4, NH3, H2, H2O) under simulated early-Earth atmosphere with electrical discharges","First creation of a living cell from non-living material","Spontaneous assembly of DNA double helix in test tubes","First synthesis of antibiotics in laboratory"],correctAnswer:"Spontaneous formation of amino acids and other organic precursors from simple inorganic gases (CH4, NH3, H2, H2O) under simulated early-Earth atmosphere with electrical discharges",rationale:{pt:"Miller-Urey experiment (Stanley Miller graduate student Harold Urey University Chicago Science 1953): closed apparatus simulating early Earth — flask CH₄ methane + NH₃ ammonia + H₂ + H₂O water vapor (Oparin-Haldane reducing atmosphere hypothesis); electrical sparks (lightning analog); cooling condenser collected products. Within ~1 week: ~5+ amino acids identified (glycine, alanine, β-alanine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid). Subsequent reanalysis 2008 Bada Johnson Lazcano Miller Cleaves Dworkin original samples + improved analytical methods → ~22+ amino acids detected. Demonstrated abiotic organic chemistry possible. Caveat: subsequent geochemical evidence early Earth atmosphere likely less reducing (CO₂/N₂/H₂O dominant) — neutralidade reduce yields → Sutherland Powner 2009 Nature cyanosulfidic chemistry alternative pathway. Concurrent: Murchison meteorite 1969 — ~80+ amino acids exogenous panspermia evidence. Origins of life chemistry field ~70 years post-Miller continuing ferment.",en:"Miller-Urey experiment (Stanley Miller graduate student of Harold Urey University Chicago Science 1953): closed apparatus simulating early Earth — flask with CH₄ methane + NH₃ ammonia + H₂ + H₂O water vapor (Oparin-Haldane reducing atmosphere hypothesis); electrical sparks (lightning analog); cooling condenser collected products. Within ~1 week: ~5+ amino acids identified (glycine, alanine, β-alanine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid). Subsequent reanalysis 2008 Bada Johnson Lazcano Miller Cleaves Dworkin of original samples + improved analytical methods → ~22+ amino acids detected. Demonstrated abiotic organic chemistry is possible. Caveat: subsequent geochemical evidence shows early Earth atmosphere likely less reducing (CO₂/N₂/H₂O dominant) — neutrality reduces yields → Sutherland/Powner 2009 Nature cyanosulfidic chemistry as alternative pathway. Concurrent: Murchison meteorite 1969 — ~80+ amino acids as exogenous/panspermia evidence. Origins-of-life chemistry field continues to ferment ~70 years post-Miller."},objectives:["Miller-Urey"]},{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"RNA world hypothesis (Gilbert 1986) e Cech/Altman ribozymes Nobel 1989 sustenta…",en:"RNA world hypothesis (Gilbert 1986) and Cech/Altman ribozymes 1989 Nobel argue…"},choices:["Early life used RNA as both genetic material and catalyst before DNA/protein, supported by ribosome RNA core (catalytic peptide-bond formation) and existence of natural ribozymes","DNA preceded RNA as the original informational molecule","Proteins existed before nucleic acids","Lipids alone could replicate without RNA or DNA"],correctAnswer:"Early life used RNA as both genetic material and catalyst before DNA/protein, supported by ribosome RNA core (catalytic peptide-bond formation) and existence of natural ribozymes",rationale:{pt:"RNA world (Walter Gilbert Nature 1986 coined nome): hipótese earliest life pre-LUCA used RNA as both informational + catalytic biopolymer — pre-protein, pre-DNA. Foundation: Cech (Tetrahymena Group I intron self-splicing 1981 PNAS) + Altman (RNase P RNA component catalytic 1983 Cell) → RNA enzymes (ribozymes) Nobel 1989 Chemistry. Subsequent: ribosome (Steitz Yale Yonath Weizmann/MPI Ramakrishnan MRC LMB X-ray crystallography Nobel 2009) — catalytic core peptidyl transferase center is RNA, not protein. Hammerhead/hairpin ribozymes natural. Aptamers, riboswitches abundant. Joyce/Bartel directed evolution → polymerase ribozymes ~95% RNA replication ability sintetizada in vitro. Bartel/Szostak/Joyce key labs. Limit RNA world: ribose synthesis abiotic challenging, RNA stability hostile, transition para DNA + protein how. Sutherland 2009 cyanosulfidic prebiotic synthesis ribose nucleotides simultaneously possible. Field active 2024+ — alternative TNA (Threose), GNA (Glycerol), PNA precursor scenarios proposed.",en:"RNA world (Walter Gilbert Nature 1986 coined the name): hypothesis that earliest life pre-LUCA used RNA as both informational + catalytic biopolymer — pre-protein, pre-DNA. Foundation: Cech (Tetrahymena Group I intron self-splicing 1981 PNAS) + Altman (RNase P RNA component catalytic 1983 Cell) → RNA enzymes (ribozymes) Nobel 1989 Chemistry. Subsequent: ribosome (Steitz Yale, Yonath Weizmann/MPI, Ramakrishnan MRC LMB X-ray crystallography Nobel 2009) — catalytic core peptidyl transferase center is RNA, not protein. Hammerhead/hairpin ribozymes are natural. Aptamers, riboswitches abundant. Joyce/Bartel directed evolution → polymerase ribozymes with ~95% RNA replication ability synthesized in vitro. Bartel/Szostak/Joyce key labs. Limits of RNA world: abiotic ribose synthesis challenging, RNA hostile to long-term stability, how the transition to DNA + protein happened. Sutherland 2009 cyanosulfidic prebiotic synthesis can produce ribose nucleotides simultaneously. Field active 2024+ — alternative TNA (Threose), GNA (Glycerol), PNA precursor scenarios proposed."},objectives:["RNA world"]},{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"LUCA (Last Universal Common Ancestor, Weiss/Martin Nat Microbiol 2016) reconstruction suggests…",en:"LUCA (Last Universal Common Ancestor, Weiss/Martin Nat Microbiol 2016) reconstruction suggests…"},choices:["Anaerobic, thermophilic, hydrogen-dependent organism with ~355 inferred core genes and reverse Krebs cycle/Wood-Ljungdahl pathway, consistent with hydrothermal vent origin","Aerobic eukaryote with mitochondria","Photosynthetic plant-like organism","Multicellular animal-like ancestor"],correctAnswer:"Anaerobic, thermophilic, hydrogen-dependent organism with ~355 inferred core genes and reverse Krebs cycle/Wood-Ljungdahl pathway, consistent with hydrothermal vent origin",rationale:{pt:'LUCA reconstruction (Weiss Sousa Mrnjavac Neukirchen Roettger Nelson-Sathi Martin Heinrich-Heine Düsseldorf Nat Microbiol 2016): comparative genomics 6.1M sequences ~2000 prokaryotes → identified ~355 protein-coding genes likely present LUCA — selected based universal presence both Bacteria + Archaea domains AND phylogenetic congruence (avoiding horizontal gene transfer false positives). LUCA features inferred: anaerobic (no O₂ enzymes), thermophilic (optimum >60°C), CO₂ + H₂ chemolithoautotroph (metabolism), nitrogen fixer (nifH NifD), Wood-Ljungdahl (reductive acetyl-CoA pathway carbon fixation), Na⁺/H⁺ gradient based motive force, reverse-Krebs partial cycle. Habitat consistent: hydrothermal vent (alkaline serpentinite Lost City type, Mike Russell hypothesis 2003+), abundant H₂ + thermal gradient + porous mineral chambers proton-Na+ gradients. Lane Martin "Vital Question" 2015 popularization. Concurrent alternative: Sutherland surface ponds (UV-driven cyanosulfidic chemistry). Field continued 2024 LUCA dating ~4.2-4.5 Ga (early after Earth formation).',en:'LUCA reconstruction (Weiss/Martin Heinrich-Heine Düsseldorf Nat Microbiol 2016): comparative genomics on 6.1M sequences from ~2000 prokaryotes → identified ~355 protein-coding genes likely present in LUCA — selected based on universal presence in both Bacteria + Archaea domains AND phylogenetic congruence (avoiding horizontal gene transfer false positives). LUCA features inferred: anaerobic (no O₂ enzymes), thermophilic (optimum >60°C), CO₂ + H₂ chemolithoautotroph (metabolism), nitrogen fixer (nifH/nifD), Wood-Ljungdahl (reductive acetyl-CoA pathway carbon fixation), Na⁺/H⁺ gradient-based motive force, reverse-Krebs partial cycle. Habitat consistent: hydrothermal vent (alkaline serpentinite Lost City type, Mike Russell hypothesis 2003+), abundant H₂ + thermal gradient + porous mineral chambers for proton-Na+ gradients. Lane/Martin "Vital Question" 2015 popularization. Concurrent alternative: Sutherland surface ponds (UV-driven cyanosulfidic chemistry). Field continued 2024 — LUCA dating ~4.2-4.5 Ga (early after Earth formation).'},objectives:["LUCA"]},{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"Endossimbiose (Lynn Margulis 1967) explica…",en:"Endosymbiosis (Lynn Margulis 1967) explains…"},choices:["Origin of mitochondria from an alpha-proteobacterium and chloroplasts from a cyanobacterium engulfed by an ancestral host cell, with their own DNA, ribosomes, and double membrane as legacy","Direct vertical inheritance of all eukaryotic organelles","Random fusion of unrelated organisms throughout history","Pure plant-animal hybridization without bacterial origin"],correctAnswer:"Origin of mitochondria from an alpha-proteobacterium and chloroplasts from a cyanobacterium engulfed by an ancestral host cell, with their own DNA, ribosomes, and double membrane as legacy",rationale:{pt:'Endosymbiotic theory (Lynn Margulis "On the origin of mitosing cells" J Theor Biol 1967): mitochondria descend from engulfed alpha-proteobacterium (Rickettsia-related) ~1.5-2 Ga ago; chloroplasts from cyanobacterium engulfed ancestral plant cell ~1 Ga. Margulis revolutionary inicialmente rejeitada — eventually validated. Evidence: organelles têm own circular DNA (similar bacterial genome, mas mostly transferred to nucleus over time, ~10-200 genes remaining), 70S bacterial-like ribosomes (vs 80S eukaryotic cytoplasmic), double membrane (inner from bacterium, outer from engulfing host phagosome), prokaryotic-like membrane lipids cardiolipin, vertical inheritance from mother (mitochondrial DNA maternally inherited mammals via egg cytoplasm). Modern phylogenetics confirms: alpha-proteobacteria ancestors mitochondria (Rickettsia closest extant relatives), cyanobacteria-like chloroplasts. Secondary/tertiary endosymbiosis: dinoflagellates/euglenoids/diatoms ate algae → algal chloroplasts (3-4 membranes, complex history). Margulis pioneered eukaryogenesis modern view. Husband Carl Sagan early career — interdisciplinary scientific household.',en:'Endosymbiotic theory (Lynn Margulis "On the origin of mitosing cells" J Theor Biol 1967): mitochondria descend from an engulfed alpha-proteobacterium (Rickettsia-related) ~1.5-2 Ga ago; chloroplasts from a cyanobacterium engulfed by an ancestral plant cell ~1 Ga. Margulis revolutionary, initially rejected — eventually validated. Evidence: organelles have their own circular DNA (similar to bacterial genome, but mostly transferred to nucleus over time, ~10-200 genes remaining), 70S bacterial-like ribosomes (vs 80S eukaryotic cytoplasmic), double membrane (inner from bacterium, outer from engulfing host phagosome), prokaryotic-like membrane lipids cardiolipin, vertical inheritance from mother (mitochondrial DNA maternally inherited in mammals via egg cytoplasm). Modern phylogenetics confirms: alpha-proteobacteria ancestors of mitochondria (Rickettsia closest extant relatives), cyanobacteria-like chloroplasts. Secondary/tertiary endosymbiosis: dinoflagellates/euglenoids/diatoms ate algae → algal chloroplasts (3-4 membranes, complex history). Margulis pioneered modern view of eukaryogenesis. Husband Carl Sagan in early career — interdisciplinary scientific household.'},objectives:["Endosymbiosis Margulis"]},{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"Asgard archaea (Ettema/Spang Nature 2015 Lokiarchaeota → Imachi 2020 cultivation) revealed…",en:"Asgard archaea (Ettema/Spang Nature 2015 Lokiarchaeota → Imachi 2020 cultivation) revealed…"},choices:["Closest known archaeal relatives of eukaryotes, with eukaryotic signature proteins (actin homologs, ESCRT, ubiquitin) supporting two-domain tree where eukaryotes emerge within archaea","Closest bacterial relatives of plants","Mammalian-specific archaea found only in human gut","Ancient rocks without biological component"],correctAnswer:"Closest known archaeal relatives of eukaryotes, with eukaryotic signature proteins (actin homologs, ESCRT, ubiquitin) supporting two-domain tree where eukaryotes emerge within archaea",rationale:{pt:'Asgard archaea: Spang Saw Jørgensen Zaremba-Niedzwiedzka Martijn Lind van Eijk Schleper Guy Ettema Uppsala Nature 2015 — discovered Lokiarchaeota deep marine sediments Loki Castle hydrothermal vent (Atlantic Mid-Ocean Ridge ~3000m). Metagenomics. Subsequent: Thorarchaeota, Odinarchaeota, Heimdallarchaeota — naming Norse gods → "Asgard" superphylum. Each lineage carries eukaryotic signature proteins (ESPs): actin/profilin/gelsolin homologs, ESCRT-III complex (membrane remodeling), ubiquitin-proteasome system, small GTPases, complex cytoskeletal regulators. Implies eukaryotic features pre-existed Asgard archaea before mitochondrial endosymbiosis → eukaryogenesis: Asgard host + alpha-proteobacterium → first eukaryote. Imachi/Nakahara/Kakumura/Itoh JAMSTEC Japan Nature 2020 — first cultivation Lokiarchaeota strain MK-D1 (12 anos cultivation effort): forms branching protrusions, syntrophic with sulfate-reducing bacteria + methanogen. Eocyte/two-domain tree (Eukaryote nested within Archaea) replaces three-domain Woese — major paradigm shift 2015-2020s. Subsequent: Hodarchaeales (Liu Hua Zhang Nature 2024) closer eukaryote root.',en:'Asgard archaea: Spang/Ettema Uppsala Nature 2015 — discovered Lokiarchaeota in deep marine sediments at Loki Castle hydrothermal vent (Atlantic Mid-Ocean Ridge ~3000 m). Metagenomics. Subsequent: Thorarchaeota, Odinarchaeota, Heimdallarchaeota — naming after Norse gods → "Asgard" superphylum. Each lineage carries eukaryotic signature proteins (ESPs): actin/profilin/gelsolin homologs, ESCRT-III complex (membrane remodeling), ubiquitin-proteasome system, small GTPases, complex cytoskeletal regulators. Implies eukaryotic features pre-existed in Asgard archaea before mitochondrial endosymbiosis → eukaryogenesis: Asgard host + alpha-proteobacterium → first eukaryote. Imachi JAMSTEC Japan Nature 2020 — first cultivation of Lokiarchaeota strain MK-D1 (12 years cultivation effort): forms branching protrusions, syntrophic with sulfate-reducing bacteria + methanogen. Eocyte/two-domain tree (Eukaryote nested within Archaea) replaces three-domain Woese — major paradigm shift 2015-2020s. Subsequent: Hodarchaeales (Liu/Hua Nature 2024) closer to eukaryote root.'},objectives:["Asgard archaea"]},{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"Three domains of life (Woese PNAS 1977) reconheceu…",en:"Three domains of life (Woese PNAS 1977) recognized…"},choices:["Archaea as a third domain distinct from Bacteria and Eukarya, based on 16S/18S rRNA sequence divergence revealing deep evolutionary distances","All life as single domain Prokarya plus Eukarya","Plants as separate domain from animals","Viruses as fourth domain alongside cellular life"],correctAnswer:"Archaea as a third domain distinct from Bacteria and Eukarya, based on 16S/18S rRNA sequence divergence revealing deep evolutionary distances",rationale:{pt:'Three domains of life (Woese Fox PNAS 1977): Carl Woese Univ Illinois pioneered ribosomal RNA (rRNA) sequencing molecular phylogenetics. 16S rRNA universally conserved → comparative ribotyping reveal deep evolution. Discovery: methanogens previously classified bacteria → completely distinct molecular signatures from typical bacteria (E. coli) — third group "Archaebacteria" (later renamed Archaea Woese 1990). Three domains: Bacteria, Archaea, Eukarya. Archaea: methanogens (Methanococcus), thermophiles (Pyrococcus), halophiles (Halobacterium), Sulfolobus. Distinct biochemistry: ether-linked lipids isoprenoid (vs ester-linked fatty acids bacteria/eukarya), absence muramic acid cell wall, RNA polymerase eukaryote-like, transcription/translation machinery archaea more similar eukaryote (TBP TATA-binding protein), histones some lineages. Decades validation. Eocyte hypothesis Lake 1980s Asgard 2015+ challenge — eukaryote emerged within Archaea (sister Asgard), redoing tree two-domain. Field paradigm shift gradual.',en:'Three domains of life (Woese/Fox PNAS 1977): Carl Woese Univ Illinois pioneered ribosomal RNA (rRNA) sequencing molecular phylogenetics. 16S rRNA universally conserved → comparative ribotyping reveals deep evolution. Discovery: methanogens previously classified as bacteria → completely distinct molecular signatures from typical bacteria (E. coli) — third group "Archaebacteria" (later renamed Archaea Woese 1990). Three domains: Bacteria, Archaea, Eukarya. Archaea: methanogens (Methanococcus), thermophiles (Pyrococcus), halophiles (Halobacterium), Sulfolobus. Distinct biochemistry: ether-linked isoprenoid lipids (vs ester-linked fatty acids in bacteria/eukarya), absence of muramic acid in cell wall, eukaryote-like RNA polymerase, transcription/translation machinery in archaea more similar to eukaryote (TBP TATA-binding protein), histones in some lineages. Decades of validation. Eocyte hypothesis Lake 1980s, Asgard 2015+ challenge — eukaryote emerged within Archaea (sister to Asgard), redoing tree to two-domain. Field paradigm shift gradual.'},objectives:["Three domains Woese"]},{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"Szostak protocell research (Harvard) approach é…",en:"Szostak protocell research (Harvard) approach is…"},choices:["Synthesis of fatty-acid vesicles encapsulating self-replicating RNA, demonstrating principles of how primitive cells could have grown, divided, and replicated genetic material chemically","Pure RNA chemistry without lipid context","3D bioprinting of bacterial cells","Photosynthesis-only biochemistry"],correctAnswer:"Synthesis of fatty-acid vesicles encapsulating self-replicating RNA, demonstrating principles of how primitive cells could have grown, divided, and replicated genetic material chemically",rationale:{pt:"Jack Szostak Harvard/MGH (Nobel Medicine 2009 telomeres separately): protocell research origins of life. Mineral-driven RNA chemistry + lipid bilayer encapsulation. Vesicles fatty acids (oleic acid, decanoic acid) — simpler bacterial diacyl phospholipids — assemble spontaneously aqueous solutions. Demonstrated: vesicles encapsulate template RNA, grow by absorbing fatty acid micelles, divide via shear forces, internal RNA non-enzymatic templated replication possible (Mansy Schrum Krishnamurthy Tobé Treco Szostak Nature 2008 — short RNA strands template-directed copy without enzyme em vesicles). Subsequent: heat cycle drives copying, bilayer permeable nucleotides but not RNA. Joint protocells + RNA world coherent narrative. Limits: ~RNA copying length-limited, fidelity issues, autopoiesis full unsolved. Field active 2024+: Sutherland Cambridge cyanosulfidic chemistry, Adamala MIT synthetic minimal cells, Krishnamurthy Scripps. Concurrent: Powner UCL prebiotic synthesis pathway. Origin chemistry meets cell biology.",en:"Jack Szostak Harvard/MGH (Nobel Medicine 2009 telomeres separately): protocell research on origins of life. Mineral-driven RNA chemistry + lipid bilayer encapsulation. Vesicles of fatty acids (oleic acid, decanoic acid) — simpler than bacterial diacyl phospholipids — assemble spontaneously in aqueous solutions. Demonstrated: vesicles encapsulate template RNA, grow by absorbing fatty acid micelles, divide via shear forces, internal RNA non-enzymatic templated replication is possible (Mansy/Szostak Nature 2008 — short RNA strands template-directed copy without enzyme in vesicles). Subsequent: heat cycle drives copying, bilayer permeable to nucleotides but not RNA. Joint protocells + RNA world give a coherent narrative. Limits: RNA copying length-limited, fidelity issues, full autopoiesis unsolved. Field active 2024+: Sutherland Cambridge cyanosulfidic chemistry, Adamala MIT synthetic minimal cells, Krishnamurthy Scripps. Concurrent: Powner UCL prebiotic synthesis pathway. Origin chemistry meets cell biology."},objectives:["Szostak protocells"]},{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"Sutherland cyanosulfidic prebiotic chemistry (Powner Sutherland Nature 2009) é…",en:"Sutherland cyanosulfidic prebiotic chemistry (Powner Sutherland Nature 2009) is…"},choices:["Plausible single-pot UV-driven synthesis of nucleotides, amino acids, and lipids from common feedstocks (HCN, H2S, formaldehyde, cyanide derivatives) under early-Earth surface conditions","Pure deep-sea chemistry without UV component","Hot-vent only synthesis without surface contribution","Synthesis exclusively requiring oxygen"],correctAnswer:"Plausible single-pot UV-driven synthesis of nucleotides, amino acids, and lipids from common feedstocks (HCN, H2S, formaldehyde, cyanide derivatives) under early-Earth surface conditions",rationale:{pt:'Cyanosulfidic chemistry (John Sutherland Cambridge / Manchester / MRC LMB → Cambridge): pre-biotic single-pot synthesis nucleotides, amino acids, e lipids precursors comum chemistry. Powner Sutherland Nature 2009 — RNA pyrimidine nucleotides synthesized via cyanide-based route bypassing problem nucleobase + ribose separate-then-link (geochemically implausible). 2015 Patel Percivalle Ritson Duffy Sutherland Nat Chem extends — same UV + HCN + H₂S chemistry produces RNA precursors + amino acids + lipid precursors em parallel. Surface ponds + UV irradiation suficiente energy. Reconciles: all building blocks single chemistry → not require multi-environment scenarios. Concurrent: Krishnamurthy Scripps (mixed thiols + nitrogen heterocycles), Joyce/Bartel ribozymes evolution. Sutherland 2024 active research replicasse RNA polymerase ribozyme + chemical replication. Lecture circuit Vital Question community. Field consensus origin life: surface plus UV (Darwin "warm little pond") OR hydrothermal vents (Russell/Lane) — both probably contributed different aspects (lipid chemistry, energy, mineral catalysis).',en:'Cyanosulfidic chemistry (John Sutherland Cambridge / Manchester / MRC LMB → Cambridge): prebiotic single-pot synthesis of nucleotide, amino acid, and lipid precursors from common chemistry. Powner/Sutherland Nature 2009 — RNA pyrimidine nucleotides synthesized via cyanide-based route bypassing the problem of nucleobase + ribose separate-then-link (geochemically implausible). 2015 Patel/Sutherland Nat Chem extends — same UV + HCN + H₂S chemistry produces RNA precursors + amino acids + lipid precursors in parallel. Surface ponds + UV irradiation provide sufficient energy. Reconciles: all building blocks from single chemistry → not requiring multi-environment scenarios. Concurrent: Krishnamurthy Scripps (mixed thiols + nitrogen heterocycles), Joyce/Bartel on ribozyme evolution. Sutherland 2024 active research on replicating RNA polymerase ribozyme + chemical replication. Lecture circuit Vital Question community. Field consensus on origin of life: surface plus UV (Darwin "warm little pond") OR hydrothermal vents (Russell/Lane) — both probably contributed different aspects (lipid chemistry, energy, mineral catalysis).'},objectives:["Cyanosulfidic Sutherland"]},{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"Snowball Earth + Cambrian explosion (Hoffman Schrag 1998) sequência é…",en:"Snowball Earth + Cambrian explosion (Hoffman/Schrag 1998) sequence is…"},choices:["Global glaciations (~720 and 635 Ma) followed by Ediacaran/Cambrian radiation of complex animal body plans, possibly linked via oxygenation and ecological release","Continuous warm climate from origin of Earth to present","Cambrian explosion preceded all glaciations","No connection between Earth climate and biological evolution"],correctAnswer:"Global glaciations (~720 and 635 Ma) followed by Ediacaran/Cambrian radiation of complex animal body plans, possibly linked via oxygenation and ecological release",rationale:{pt:"Snowball Earth (Paul Hoffman Harvard + Daniel Schrag Harvard Science 1998): Neoproterozoic glaciations Sturtian (~720-660 Ma) + Marinoan (~650-635 Ma) globally extensive — geological evidence cap carbonates, glacial deposits low latitudes, ice-rafted dropstones tropical Brazil/Australia/Namibia. Hipótese: complete or near-complete ice cover (equator-to-pole), albedo runaway, end via volcanic CO₂ accumulation. Subsequent: Ediacaran biota ~600-540 Ma — first complex multicellular organisms (Dickinsonia, Charnia bizarre body plans). Cambrian explosion ~541 Ma — rapid diversification animal phyla (~50M years majority modern phyla establish). Possible linkages: post-glacial ocean fertilization + atmospheric O₂ rise (~21% modern) → eukaryotic respiration efficient → metazoan complexity afford. Predator-prey arms race genetic regulatory evolution. Subsequent NASA Astrobiology + Earth System Science 2020s — Snowball/Eukaryogenesis/Cambrian linked transitions Earth surface biosphere coevolution. Lenton Imperial bauplans development. Active research Earth-life coevolution 2024+.",en:"Snowball Earth (Paul Hoffman Harvard + Daniel Schrag Harvard Science 1998): Neoproterozoic glaciations Sturtian (~720-660 Ma) + Marinoan (~650-635 Ma) globally extensive — geological evidence: cap carbonates, glacial deposits at low latitudes, ice-rafted dropstones in tropical Brazil/Australia/Namibia. Hypothesis: complete or near-complete ice cover (equator-to-pole), albedo runaway, end via volcanic CO₂ accumulation. Subsequent: Ediacaran biota ~600-540 Ma — first complex multicellular organisms (Dickinsonia, Charnia bizarre body plans). Cambrian explosion ~541 Ma — rapid diversification of animal phyla (in ~50M years majority of modern phyla established). Possible linkages: post-glacial ocean fertilization + atmospheric O₂ rise (~21% modern) → efficient eukaryotic respiration → metazoan complexity affordable. Predator-prey arms race driving genetic regulatory evolution. Subsequent NASA Astrobiology + Earth System Science 2020s — Snowball/Eukaryogenesis/Cambrian as linked Earth-surface biosphere coevolution transitions. Active research on Earth-life coevolution 2024+."},objectives:["Snowball Earth Cambrian"]},{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"Hydrothermal vent hypothesis origins (Russell Hall Scotland 1990s + Martin Lane Düsseldorf) propôs…",en:"Hydrothermal vent hypothesis (Russell Hall Scotland 1990s + Martin/Lane Düsseldorf) proposed…"},choices:["Alkaline serpentinite hydrothermal vents (e.g., Lost City type) provide proton gradients across mineral chambers, energy for chemiosmotic metabolism prefiguring LUCA-like organisms","Black smoker only — high temperature + acidic fluid as origin","Surface lakes only without any vent contribution","Atmospheric chemistry only without water involvement"],correctAnswer:"Alkaline serpentinite hydrothermal vents (e.g., Lost City type) provide proton gradients across mineral chambers, energy for chemiosmotic metabolism prefiguring LUCA-like organisms",rationale:{pt:'Alkaline hydrothermal vent (AHV) hypothesis: Mike Russell Glasgow 1989 + Wächtershäuser surface metabolism alternative. Russell Hall 1997 emphasized Lost City-type vents (discovered 2000 Atlantis Massif Atlantic, Kelley) — alkaline (pH ~9-11), warm (40-90°C, not 350°C black smokers), serpentinite-water reaction H₂ producing. Mineral chimneys porous (Mg/Fe carbonates + brucite + serpentine) — abundant pore gradients ~10-100µm structures. Naturally generated proton (H⁺) gradient via alkaline interior + acidic ocean exterior → primitive chemiosmotic energy currency drives reductive carbon fixation (Wood-Ljungdahl pathway). FeS, NiS minerals catalyze reductive chemistry CO₂ → acetate → biology precursors. Subsequent Nick Lane "Vital Question" 2015 popularization. William Martin Düsseldorf central proponent. LUCA reconstruction Weiss Martin 2016 consistent vent origin. Concurrent debate Sutherland surface/UV. Mike Russell career-spanning advocate. Field 2024 lab experiments simulating vent geochemistry (Smithsonian, JPL, ENS Paris).',en:'Alkaline hydrothermal vent (AHV) hypothesis: Mike Russell Glasgow 1989 + Wächtershäuser surface metabolism alternative. Russell/Hall 1997 emphasized Lost City-type vents (discovered 2000 Atlantis Massif Atlantic, Kelley) — alkaline (pH ~9-11), warm (40-90°C, not 350°C black smokers), produced via serpentinite-water reaction generating H₂. Mineral chimneys are porous (Mg/Fe carbonates + brucite + serpentine) — abundant pore gradients on ~10-100 µm structures. Naturally generated proton (H⁺) gradient via alkaline interior + acidic ocean exterior → primitive chemiosmotic energy currency drives reductive carbon fixation (Wood-Ljungdahl pathway). FeS, NiS minerals catalyze reductive chemistry CO₂ → acetate → biology precursors. Subsequent Nick Lane "Vital Question" 2015 popularization. William Martin Düsseldorf central proponent. LUCA reconstruction Weiss/Martin 2016 consistent with vent origin. Concurrent debate with Sutherland surface/UV. Mike Russell career-spanning advocate. Field 2024 lab experiments simulating vent geochemistry (Smithsonian, JPL, ENS Paris).'},objectives:["Hydrothermal vent origin"]}]},{title:{pt:"Página 2 — Astrobiologia e DNA antigo",en:"Page 2 — Astrobiology and ancient DNA"},exercises:[{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"Mars Perseverance rover (NASA Jezero Crater feb 2021) primary mission é…",en:"Mars Perseverance rover (NASA Jezero Crater Feb 2021) primary mission is…"},choices:["Search for biosignatures in ancient lakebed sediments and cache rock cores for future Mars Sample Return mission to Earth analysis","Establishing first human Mars base","Exclusively imaging Martian surface without sample collection","Pure atmospheric chemistry without surface investigation"],correctAnswer:"Search for biosignatures in ancient lakebed sediments and cache rock cores for future Mars Sample Return mission to Earth analysis",rationale:{pt:'Mars Perseverance rover (NASA, lançado julho 2020, landed Jezero Crater Mars 18 fevereiro 2021): primary mission astrobiology — search ancient biosignatures in 3.5+ Ga lakebed/river-delta sediments. Jezero: ancient lake/delta paleoenvironment, well-preserved layered sediments. Instruments: PIXL X-ray (chemical mapping), SHERLOC UV Raman + autofluorescence (organics), SuperCam laser-LIBS (composition), Mastcam-Z imaging, MOXIE oxygen production from CO₂ (technology demo). Sample caching: ~38 cores collected → tubes deposited "depot" Three Forks site → future Mars Sample Return (MSR) mission (NASA + ESA, planned ~2030s, $11B budget challenges 2024) retrieve samples → Earth analysis under planetary protection lab. Significant findings: organic molecules detected (carbonaceous), sulfate veins, lake-bed sediments confirmed, possible biosignatures debated (sample SamGPT-like cells under Sherloc). Helicopter Ingenuity ~72 flights over 2+ years (record deceased março 2024 rotor damage). Chinese mission Zhurong Tianwen-1 2021-2023 parallel.',en:'Mars Perseverance rover (NASA, launched July 2020, landed in Jezero Crater Mars 18 February 2021): primary mission astrobiology — search for ancient biosignatures in 3.5+ Ga lakebed/river-delta sediments. Jezero: ancient lake/delta paleoenvironment, well-preserved layered sediments. Instruments: PIXL X-ray (chemical mapping), SHERLOC UV Raman + autofluorescence (organics), SuperCam laser-LIBS (composition), Mastcam-Z imaging, MOXIE oxygen production from CO₂ (technology demo). Sample caching: ~38 cores collected → tubes deposited at "depot" Three Forks site → future Mars Sample Return (MSR) mission (NASA + ESA, planned ~2030s, $11B budget challenges 2024) to retrieve samples → Earth analysis under planetary protection lab. Significant findings: organic molecules detected (carbonaceous), sulfate veins, lake-bed sediments confirmed, possible biosignatures debated. Helicopter Ingenuity ~72 flights over 2+ years (record, deceased March 2024 due to rotor damage). Chinese mission Zhurong Tianwen-1 2021-2023 in parallel.'},objectives:["Perseverance Mars"]},{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"Europa Clipper (NASA outubro 2024 launch) target é…",en:"Europa Clipper (NASA October 2024 launch) target is…"},choices:["Jupiter moon Europa with subsurface ocean beneath ~10-30 km ice shell — characterize habitability via flyby radar/spectroscopy/magnetometry, arriving 2030","Mars exclusively","Saturn ring system without moon focus","Earth's own moon"],correctAnswer:"Jupiter moon Europa with subsurface ocean beneath ~10-30 km ice shell — characterize habitability via flyby radar/spectroscopy/magnetometry, arriving 2030",rationale:{pt:"Europa Clipper (NASA flagship mission ~$5.2B): launched 14 outubro 2024 SpaceX Falcon Heavy from Kennedy Space Center → trajectory Mars-Jupiter gravity assist → Jupiter orbit insertion abril 2030. Will perform ~49 flybys Europa over 4 anos (reside in Jupiter orbit, not Europa orbit due Jupiter radiation belt damage electronics). Target Europa: Jupiter moon, evidence subsurface ocean beneath ~10-30km ice shell — Galileo magnetometer 1990s induced field signal, Hubble plumes possibly water vapor, surface chaos terrain young (~50-100 Ma) suggesting active resurfacing. Goal: characterize habitability — three pillars NASA astrobiology — water (confirm subsurface ocean depth + characteristics), chemistry (organic compounds, redox energy), energy (radiogenic + tidal heating). Instruments: ice-penetrating radar REASON, magnetometer ECM, mass spectrometer, IR spectrometer, narrow-angle camera. Concurrent: ESA JUICE mission Jupiter system 2023-2031 will study Europa, Ganymede (eventual orbit), Callisto. Decade-defining astrobiology missions. Also planned: Dragonfly Titan ~2034 (rotorcraft).",en:"Europa Clipper (NASA flagship mission ~$5.2B): launched 14 October 2024 on SpaceX Falcon Heavy from Kennedy Space Center → trajectory Mars-Jupiter gravity assist → Jupiter orbit insertion April 2030. Will perform ~49 flybys of Europa over 4 years (resides in Jupiter orbit, not Europa orbit due to Jupiter radiation belt damage to electronics). Target Europa: Jupiter moon, evidence of subsurface ocean beneath ~10-30 km ice shell — Galileo magnetometer 1990s induced field signal, Hubble plumes possibly water vapor, young surface chaos terrain (~50-100 Ma) suggesting active resurfacing. Goal: characterize habitability — three pillars of NASA astrobiology — water (confirm subsurface ocean depth + characteristics), chemistry (organic compounds, redox energy), energy (radiogenic + tidal heating). Instruments: ice-penetrating radar REASON, magnetometer ECM, mass spectrometer, IR spectrometer, narrow-angle camera. Concurrent: ESA JUICE mission Jupiter system 2023-2031 will study Europa, Ganymede (eventual orbit), Callisto. Decade-defining astrobiology missions. Also planned: Dragonfly Titan ~2034 (rotorcraft)."},objectives:["Europa Clipper"]},{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"Enceladus plumes (Cassini 2005-2017) revealed…",en:"Enceladus plumes (Cassini 2005-2017) revealed…"},choices:["Saturn moon Enceladus ejecting water vapor + ice grains + organic compounds + H2 from a subsurface ocean accessible via south polar geysers, presenting accessible astrobiology target","Pure rock surface without volatiles","No subsurface water on any Saturn moon","Methane lake similar to Titan"],correctAnswer:"Saturn moon Enceladus ejecting water vapor + ice grains + organic compounds + H2 from a subsurface ocean accessible via south polar geysers, presenting accessible astrobiology target",rationale:{pt:'Enceladus (Saturn moon ~500km diameter): Cassini-Huygens NASA-ESA mission 2004-2017 revealed extraordinary world. 2005 — south polar plumes detected: water vapor + ice grains + organic compounds (~40 species methane, propane, formaldehyde, HCN, complex organics) + H₂ molecular hydrogen + silica nanoparticles + salts (NaCl). Source: subsurface ocean (~10km thick) beneath ~25-40km ice crust, south polar fissures "tiger stripes" geysers. Hydrothermal activity inferred: H₂ + silica → consistent serpentinite-driven warm vents on rocky core. Cassini flew through plumes multiple times, sampled directly mass spectrometers. Habitability candidates: liquid water + organic chemistry + redox energy + hydrothermal heat. Subsequent: Hsu Postberg Sekine Saito Saito Wenz Bertini Hilchenbach Klenner Kempf Karkoschka Murray Schwartz Burkhart Cordier Bukulin Strazzulla Sicardy Müller Spahn Kempf Postberg Trumbo Hesse Kempf Kempf Postberg Glein Lunine Kuwahara Sekine Schubert Helbert Kempf Postberg Cassini ECDA evidence phosphates 2023 Nature — completes CHNOPS biology requirements. Future missions Enceladus Orbiter Lander not yet selected — Europa-priority. Field considers Enceladus possibly more accessible biosignature search Mars/Europa plumes-flyby strategy.',en:'Enceladus (Saturn moon ~500 km diameter): Cassini-Huygens NASA-ESA mission 2004-2017 revealed an extraordinary world. 2005 — south polar plumes detected: water vapor + ice grains + organic compounds (~40 species methane, propane, formaldehyde, HCN, complex organics) + H₂ molecular hydrogen + silica nanoparticles + salts (NaCl). Source: subsurface ocean (~10 km thick) beneath ~25-40 km ice crust, south polar fissures "tiger stripes" geysers. Hydrothermal activity inferred: H₂ + silica → consistent with serpentinite-driven warm vents on rocky core. Cassini flew through plumes multiple times, sampling directly with mass spectrometers. Habitability candidates: liquid water + organic chemistry + redox energy + hydrothermal heat. Subsequent: 2023 Nature evidence of phosphates — completes CHNOPS biology requirements. Future missions Enceladus Orbiter Lander not yet selected — Europa-priority. Field considers Enceladus possibly more accessible biosignature search than Mars/Europa via plume-flyby strategy.'},objectives:["Enceladus plumes"]},{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"Titan / Cassini-Huygens 2005 + Dragonfly mission é…",en:"Titan / Cassini-Huygens 2005 + Dragonfly mission is…"},choices:["Saturn moon with thick N2/methane atmosphere, methane/ethane lakes, complex organic chemistry possibly resembling pre-biotic Earth; Dragonfly rotorcraft ~2034 will explore","Mars equivalent without atmosphere","Identical to Earth's moon geologically","Pure ice surface without atmosphere or organics"],correctAnswer:"Saturn moon with thick N2/methane atmosphere, methane/ethane lakes, complex organic chemistry possibly resembling pre-biotic Earth; Dragonfly rotorcraft ~2034 will explore",rationale:{pt:"Titan (Saturn largest moon ~5150km diameter, larger Mercury): Cassini-Huygens NASA-ESA mission 2004-2017. Huygens probe descent 14 janeiro 2005 — successful landing Titan surface, first outer solar system. Findings: thick atmosphere ~95% N₂ + ~5% CH₄ methane (only moon Solar System substantial atmosphere), surface pressure ~1.45atm, temperature ~94K (-179°C). Methane/ethane hydrocarbon lakes/seas (Ligeia Mare, Kraken Mare ~1100km Hawaii equivalent) — only stable surface liquid Solar System besides Earth. Methane cycle: rain-evaporate-rain. Cryovolcanism likely. Complex tholin organics atmospheric haze (tholin = nitrogen-containing complex organic Sagan term 1979 Carl Sagan studies). Subsurface water-ice ocean confirmed. Possibilities astrobiology: methane-based exotic chemistry, water-ammonia subsurface ocean, organic-rich surface possibly preserve prebiotic chemistry frozen. Dragonfly mission (NASA New Frontiers): rotorcraft launching ~2027, arriving Titan ~2034, ~32-month surface mission 8-rotor flight covering ~175km Selk crater region. Field astrobiology Titan complementary: prebiotic chemistry analog rather than active biology candidate.",en:"Titan (Saturn largest moon ~5150 km diameter, larger than Mercury): Cassini-Huygens NASA-ESA mission 2004-2017. Huygens probe descent 14 January 2005 — successful landing on Titan surface, first in outer solar system. Findings: thick atmosphere ~95% N₂ + ~5% CH₄ methane (only moon in Solar System with substantial atmosphere), surface pressure ~1.45 atm, temperature ~94 K (-179 °C). Methane/ethane hydrocarbon lakes/seas (Ligeia Mare, Kraken Mare ~1100 km Hawaii-equivalent) — only stable surface liquid in Solar System besides Earth. Methane cycle: rain-evaporate-rain. Cryovolcanism likely. Complex tholin organics in atmospheric haze (tholin = nitrogen-containing complex organic, Sagan term 1979). Subsurface water-ice ocean confirmed. Possibilities for astrobiology: methane-based exotic chemistry, water-ammonia subsurface ocean, organic-rich surface possibly preserving prebiotic chemistry frozen. Dragonfly mission (NASA New Frontiers): rotorcraft launching ~2027, arriving Titan ~2034, ~32-month surface mission with 8-rotor flight covering ~175 km Selk crater region. Field astrobiology of Titan complementary: prebiotic chemistry analog rather than active biology candidate."},objectives:["Titan Dragonfly"]},{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"Extremophiles (Deinococcus radiodurans, Pyrococcus furiosus, tardigrades) demonstrate…",en:"Extremophiles (Deinococcus radiodurans, Pyrococcus furiosus, tardigrades) demonstrate…"},choices:["Life can survive extreme conditions — high radiation, temperatures up to ~120°C, pH ~0-12, vacuum, desiccation — expanding the range of habitability for astrobiology","Life requires only Earth-surface mesophilic conditions","Extremophiles are Earth-only and irrelevant to astrobiology","Tardigrades are mythological organisms not real"],correctAnswer:"Life can survive extreme conditions — high radiation, temperatures up to ~120°C, pH ~0-12, vacuum, desiccation — expanding the range of habitability for astrobiology",rationale:{pt:"Extremophiles: Deinococcus radiodurans (Anderson 1956 Oregon Agricultural Experiment Station): radio-resistant bacterium ~5000-15000 Gy γ-radiation tolerated (humans lethal ~5Gy). Mechanism: efficient DNA repair (~RecA homologous recombination), Mn²⁺ accumulation protect proteins ROS, polyploid (~10 genome copies template repair). Pyrococcus furiosus archaeon ~100°C optimum, hyperthermophile. Thermus aquaticus discovered Yellowstone ~1969 Brock — Taq DNA polymerase enabled PCR (Mullis Nobel 1993). Halobacterium salinarum NaCl saturated brines. Acidithiobacillus pH ~0. Picrophilus oshimae pH 0-3.5. Snottites em mineworkings extreme acidity. Tardigrades (water bears Phylum Tardigrada): cryptobiosis — anhydrobiosis (desiccation), radioresistance, vacuum survival (Jönsson 2008 Nature space exposure 10 dias). Trehalose disaccharide replaces water cells. Implications astrobiology: range conditions life sustain expand search criteria (Mars surface UV-resistant subsurface, Europa ocean extremes, Enceladus high-salt basaltic). Field 2024 active: panspermia debate, Galápagos vent biology, deep biosphere kilometers subsurface microbes (~10²⁹ cells biomass).",en:"Extremophiles: Deinococcus radiodurans (Anderson 1956 Oregon Agricultural Experiment Station): radio-resistant bacterium ~5000-15000 Gy γ-radiation tolerated (humans lethal at ~5 Gy). Mechanism: efficient DNA repair (~RecA homologous recombination), Mn²⁺ accumulation protects proteins from ROS, polyploid (~10 genome copies for template repair). Pyrococcus furiosus archaeon ~100 °C optimum, hyperthermophile. Thermus aquaticus discovered Yellowstone ~1969 Brock — Taq DNA polymerase enabled PCR (Mullis Nobel 1993). Halobacterium salinarum thrives in NaCl-saturated brines. Acidithiobacillus pH ~0. Picrophilus oshimae pH 0-3.5. Tardigrades (water bears Phylum Tardigrada): cryptobiosis — anhydrobiosis (desiccation), radioresistance, vacuum survival (Jönsson 2008 Nature space exposure 10 days). Trehalose disaccharide replaces water in cells. Implications for astrobiology: range of conditions life sustains expands search criteria (Mars surface UV-resistant subsurface, Europa ocean extremes, Enceladus high-salt basaltic). Field 2024 active: panspermia debate, Galápagos vent biology, deep-biosphere kilometers subsurface microbes (~10²⁹ cells biomass)."},objectives:["Extremophiles"]},{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"JWST K2-18b atmosphere DMS detection (Madhusudhan Cambridge Sept 2023) é…",en:"JWST K2-18b atmosphere DMS detection (Madhusudhan Cambridge Sept 2023) is…"},choices:["Tentative dimethyl sulfide (DMS) signal in a K2-18b sub-Neptune exoplanet atmosphere — DMS is a potential biosignature on Earth produced by phytoplankton, requiring confirmation","Definitive proof of life on K2-18b","Pure rock identification without atmosphere","Detection of an Earth-twin planet"],correctAnswer:"Tentative dimethyl sulfide (DMS) signal in a K2-18b sub-Neptune exoplanet atmosphere — DMS is a potential biosignature on Earth produced by phytoplankton, requiring confirmation",rationale:{pt:'K2-18b exoplanet atmosphere (Madhusudhan Sarkar Constantinou Holmberg Piette Moses ApJL Sept 2023): JWST NIRISS NIRSpec MIRI transmission spectroscopy K2-18b — sub-Neptune (~8.6 Earth masses ~2.6 Earth radii) M-dwarf host star ~110 light years away. Detected: methane (CH₄), CO₂. Tentative ~3σ signal dimethyl sulfide (DMS, (CH₃)₂S) — terrestrial DMS produced almost exclusively biology (phytoplankton, marine algae) → potencial biosignature. Authors propose K2-18b "Hycean" world (hydrogen-rich atmosphere + liquid water ocean). Major caveats: DMS detection ~3σ inicial não confirmed (>5σ standard solid claim), abiotic DMS production possible (Reed Cell 2024 lab demonstration), models alternative compositions fit. Subsequent analyses 2024 Madhusudhan revisão + crítica from Schmidt Sci Adv 2024 — disputed signal. Atmospheric biosignatures generally complex: O₂ + O₃ + CH₄ disequilibrium classical Lovelock criterion. Other targets: TRAPPIST-1 system 7 Earth-sized (JWST observations 2023+). Field rapidly evolving 2024+. Future missions: Habitable Worlds Observatory (HWO) NASA 2030s direct imaging Earth-like.',en:'K2-18b exoplanet atmosphere (Madhusudhan/Cambridge ApJL Sept 2023): JWST NIRISS/NIRSpec/MIRI transmission spectroscopy of K2-18b — sub-Neptune (~8.6 Earth masses, ~2.6 Earth radii) around M-dwarf host star ~110 light years away. Detected: methane (CH₄), CO₂. Tentative ~3σ signal of dimethyl sulfide (DMS, (CH₃)₂S) — terrestrial DMS is produced almost exclusively by biology (phytoplankton, marine algae) → potential biosignature. Authors propose K2-18b is a "Hycean" world (hydrogen-rich atmosphere + liquid water ocean). Major caveats: DMS detection ~3σ initial, not confirmed (>5σ standard for solid claim), abiotic DMS production possible (Reed Cell 2024 lab demonstration), alternative model compositions fit. Subsequent analyses 2024 Madhusudhan revisited + critique from Schmidt Sci Adv 2024 — disputed signal. Atmospheric biosignatures generally complex: O₂ + O₃ + CH₄ disequilibrium classical Lovelock criterion. Other targets: TRAPPIST-1 system 7 Earth-sized (JWST observations 2023+). Field rapidly evolving 2024+. Future missions: Habitable Worlds Observatory (HWO) NASA 2030s direct imaging Earth-like.'},objectives:["K2-18b biosignature"]},{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"Neanderthal genome (Green/Pääbo Science 2010) revelou…",en:"Neanderthal genome (Green/Pääbo Science 2010) revealed…"},choices:["~1.3-2.6% Neanderthal DNA in non-African modern humans, indicating interbreeding ~50-60 ka ago in Eurasia after H. sapiens out-of-Africa migration","Total separation between Neanderthals and modern humans","50% Neanderthal DNA in all humans","No evidence of any genetic mixing"],correctAnswer:"~1.3-2.6% Neanderthal DNA in non-African modern humans, indicating interbreeding ~50-60 ka ago in Eurasia after H. sapiens out-of-Africa migration",rationale:{pt:"Neanderthal genome (Green Krause Briggs Maricic Stenzel Kircher Patterson Li Zhai Fritz Hansen Durand Malaspinas Jensen Marques-Bonet Alkan Prüfer Meyer Burbano Good Schultz Aximu-Petri Butthof Höber Höffner Siegemund Weihmann Nusbaum Lander Russ Novod Affourtit Egholm Verna Rudan Brajkovic Kucan Gušic Doronichev Golovanova Lalueza-Fox de la Rasilla Fortea Rosas Schmitz Johnson Eichler Falush Birney Mullikin Slatkin Nielsen Kelso Lachmann Reich Pääbo Max Planck/Broad/UCSC Science 2010 maio): primeira draft Neanderthal genome (~1.3x coverage) sequenced from ~38000-year-old bone fragments Vindija cave Croatia. Compared modern human genomes (Yoruba African, French European, Han Chinese, Papuan): demonstrated non-African humans contain ~1.3-2.6% Neanderthal DNA, Africans (~0%). Implies interbreeding occurred ~50-60 ka in Middle East after Homo sapiens out-of-Africa migration encountered Neanderthals. Subsequent: 2014 high-coverage Altai Neanderthal genome (Prüfer Nature), Chagyrskaya genome 2020, Mezmaiskaya Caucasus female complete X 2024. Functional consequences: Neanderthal-derived alleles affect immunity (HLA, OAS interferon), skin/hair (BNC2), depression risk, COVID-19 severity (Zeberg/Pääbo 2020 Nature chromosome 3 region). Subsequently introgression narrative refined: multiple events, both sides extinct + survival.",en:"Neanderthal genome (Green/Pääbo Max Planck/Broad/UCSC Science May 2010): first draft Neanderthal genome (~1.3x coverage) sequenced from ~38000-year-old bone fragments Vindija cave Croatia. Compared with modern human genomes (Yoruba African, French European, Han Chinese, Papuan): demonstrated non-African humans contain ~1.3-2.6% Neanderthal DNA, Africans (~0%). Implies interbreeding occurred ~50-60 ka in the Middle East after Homo sapiens out-of-Africa migration encountered Neanderthals. Subsequent: 2014 high-coverage Altai Neanderthal genome (Prüfer Nature), Chagyrskaya genome 2020, Mezmaiskaya Caucasus female complete X 2024. Functional consequences: Neanderthal-derived alleles affect immunity (HLA, OAS interferon), skin/hair (BNC2), depression risk, COVID-19 severity (Zeberg/Pääbo 2020 Nature chromosome 3 region). Subsequently introgression narrative refined: multiple events, both extinct sides + survival."},objectives:["Neanderthal genome"]},{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"Denisovans (Pääbo/Reich 2010 Nature finger bone) descoberta foi…",en:"Denisovans (Pääbo/Reich 2010 Nature finger bone) discovery was…"},choices:["New extinct hominin lineage identified entirely from genomic analysis of a finger bone in Denisova cave Siberia, with ~3-6% ancestry in modern Melanesians/Papuans","First archaeological skeleton of a Neanderthal","Modern human population in Africa","First mammoth genome sequencing"],correctAnswer:"New extinct hominin lineage identified entirely from genomic analysis of a finger bone in Denisova cave Siberia, with ~3-6% ancestry in modern Melanesians/Papuans",rationale:{pt:"Denisovans (Krause Fu Good Viola Shunkov Derevianko Pääbo Max Planck Nature 2010 dezembro): genome sequenced from finger bone (~50ka) discovered Denisova cave Altai Mountains Siberia, originally thought Neanderthal/H. sapiens. Genomic analysis: distinct lineage (sister Neanderthals, both diverging H. sapiens common ancestor ~600 ka). No diagnostic skeletal remains identified initially — entirely genome-defined species. Subsequent: ~6 specimens identified Denisova cave + Tibetan plateau Baishiya (Xiahe mandible 2019 ~160 ka), Cobra cave Laos 2024 (proteomic identification). Distribution: presumed throughout East Asia mid/late-Pleistocene. Modern human admixture: ~3-6% Melanesian/Papuan, ~0.2% mainland East Asian + Native American. Functional inheritance: EPAS1 high-altitude adaptation Tibetans (Huerta-Sánchez Nature 2014 — Denisovan-derived EPAS1 variant ~80% Tibetans, ~9% Han Chinese — adaptation hypoxia ~3500m altitude). Other Denisovan-influenced traits: skin pigmentation, immunity, cold adaptation. Composite hominin Denny (Slon Nature 2018) — Neanderthal mother + Denisovan father F1 individual found Denisova cave, demonstrates active interbreeding contemporaneous.",en:"Denisovans (Krause/Pääbo Max Planck Nature December 2010): genome sequenced from a finger bone (~50 ka) discovered in Denisova cave Altai Mountains Siberia, originally thought to be Neanderthal/H. sapiens. Genomic analysis: distinct lineage (sister to Neanderthals, both diverging from H. sapiens common ancestor ~600 ka). No diagnostic skeletal remains identified initially — entirely genome-defined species. Subsequent: ~6 specimens identified in Denisova cave + Tibetan plateau Baishiya (Xiahe mandible 2019 ~160 ka), Cobra cave Laos 2024 (proteomic identification). Distribution: presumed throughout East Asia in mid/late-Pleistocene. Modern human admixture: ~3-6% in Melanesians/Papuans, ~0.2% in mainland East Asians + Native Americans. Functional inheritance: EPAS1 high-altitude adaptation in Tibetans (Huerta-Sánchez Nature 2014 — Denisovan-derived EPAS1 variant in ~80% of Tibetans, ~9% of Han Chinese — adaptation to hypoxia at ~3500 m altitude). Other Denisovan-influenced traits: skin pigmentation, immunity, cold adaptation. Composite hominin Denny (Slon Nature 2018) — Neanderthal mother + Denisovan father, F1 individual found in Denisova cave, demonstrates active contemporaneous interbreeding."},objectives:["Denisovans"]},{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"Ancient DNA from sediments (Slon/Meyer Pääbo 2017+) técnica é…",en:"Ancient DNA from sediments (Slon/Meyer Pääbo 2017+) technique is…"},choices:["Recovery of hominin and animal DNA directly from cave sediments without bones, by capturing DNA from sediment particles via hybridization, expanding ancient DNA accessibility","Standard skeletal DNA extraction only","Pure modern environmental DNA (eDNA) sampling","Synthetic DNA generation in laboratory"],correctAnswer:"Recovery of hominin and animal DNA directly from cave sediments without bones, by capturing DNA from sediment particles via hybridization, expanding ancient DNA accessibility",rationale:{pt:"Ancient sediment DNA (sedaDNA): Slon Hopfe Weiss Mafessoni Henter Steinrücken Filippi Lichtenberg Talamo Krajcarz Aximu-Petri Nicholson Aspell Eshlimani Wassermann Nagel Brattain Brock Casbon Mota Nicholson Berry Brock Higham Renaud Möller Jagannath Plinge Prüfer Meyer Slatkin Pääbo Max Planck Science 2017: recovery hominin (Neanderthal, Denisovan) DNA directly cave sediment matrix sem identifiable bone fragments. Methodology: extract DNA sediments → enrichment hybridization capture (mitochondrial DNA bait kit), high-throughput sequencing, computational decontamination + species assignment. Demonstrated: Denisova cave sediments contain H. sapiens, Neanderthals, mammoth, woolly rhinoceros DNA. Subsequent expansion: nuclear DNA from sediments (Vernot Zavala Gómez-Olivencia Slon Petr Mafessoni Skov Macfarlane Cocoeleau Schroeder Renaud Pääbo Meyer Science 2021) — first nuclear DNA Neanderthal sediments. Applications: study sites without preserved bones (most Paleolithic sites!), broader geographic coverage. Concurrent Permafrost ancient DNA (Pedersen 2016 Nature plants animals 700ka mammoth steppe), gut microbiomes ancient (Wibowo Polish coprolites 2021), 1+ Mya recovered DNA (Kjær Wisthaler permafrost).",en:"Ancient sediment DNA (sedaDNA): Slon/Pääbo Max Planck Science 2017: recovery of hominin (Neanderthal, Denisovan) DNA directly from cave sediment matrix without identifiable bone fragments. Methodology: extract DNA from sediments → enrichment hybridization capture (mitochondrial DNA bait kit), high-throughput sequencing, computational decontamination + species assignment. Demonstrated: Denisova cave sediments contain H. sapiens, Neanderthal, mammoth, woolly rhinoceros DNA. Subsequent expansion: nuclear DNA from sediments (Vernot/Pääbo/Meyer Science 2021) — first nuclear DNA of Neanderthal from sediments. Applications: study sites without preserved bones (most Paleolithic sites!), broader geographic coverage. Concurrent permafrost ancient DNA (Pedersen 2016 Nature plants animals 700 ka mammoth steppe), ancient gut microbiomes (Wibowo Polish coprolites 2021), 1+ Mya recovered DNA (Kjær permafrost)."},objectives:["sedaDNA"]},{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"Reich Harvard ancient DNA paleogenomics scaling 2010s+ é…",en:"Reich Harvard ancient DNA paleogenomics scaling 2010s+ is…"},choices:["Expansion to ~10000+ ancient genomes globally enabling fine-scale population history reconstruction including agricultural expansions, Bronze Age migrations, and human-population structure","Single-sample academic curiosity without scale","Limited to 10 ancient samples worldwide","Pure modern DNA without ancient component"],correctAnswer:"Expansion to ~10000+ ancient genomes globally enabling fine-scale population history reconstruction including agricultural expansions, Bronze Age migrations, and human-population structure",rationale:{pt:'David Reich Harvard Med School + Broad Institute: paleogenomics scaling. Followed Pääbo methodological foundation. Reich lab (with Iosif Lazaridis Niall Patterson Daniel Olalde Mark Lipson) industrial-scale: ~10000+ ancient genomes globally. Major findings: Yamnaya steppe expansion ~5000 BP carrying Indo-European languages Europe (Allentoft Sikora et al Nature 2015 Haak Lazaridis Lipson Reich Nature 2015), Anatolian Neolithic farmers ~8000 BP spread Europe, multiple back-migrations Asia, Native Americans single major migration ~15000 BP from Beringia. African ancient DNA: Skoglund Lazaridis Mathieson Mallick Reich Cell 2017 deep population structure pre-agricultural. South Asia: Narasimhan Patterson Moorjani Lazaridis Lipson Mark Reich Science 2019 — Steppe ancestry Indus Valley Civilization → modern Indians composite. Native Americans Posth Nature 2018 Reich Cell 2018 fine-scale. New Zealand Maori 2018, Polynesia 2021. Caribbean 2020 multiple ancestries Lipson 2024. Reich book "Who We Are and How We Got Here" 2018 popularization. Field 2024+ continues — protein-coding selection signals, archaic introgression, deep-time expansion narratives.',en:'David Reich Harvard Med School + Broad Institute: paleogenomics scaling. Followed Pääbo methodological foundation. Reich lab industrial-scale: ~10000+ ancient genomes globally. Major findings: Yamnaya steppe expansion ~5000 BP carrying Indo-European languages into Europe (Allentoft Nature 2015, Haak/Reich Nature 2015), Anatolian Neolithic farmers ~8000 BP spread into Europe, multiple back-migrations into Asia, Native Americans single major migration ~15000 BP from Beringia. African ancient DNA: Skoglund/Reich Cell 2017 deep population structure pre-agricultural. South Asia: Narasimhan/Reich Science 2019 — Steppe ancestry + Indus Valley Civilization → modern Indians composite. Native Americans Posth Nature 2018 + Reich Cell 2018 fine-scale. New Zealand Maori 2018, Polynesia 2021. Reich book "Who We Are and How We Got Here" 2018 popularization. Field 2024+ continues — protein-coding selection signals, archaic introgression, deep-time expansion narratives.'},objectives:["Reich paleogenomics"]}]}],target:20},a=e.title,i=e.level,r=e.subject,n=e.objectives,o=e.difficulty,t=e.authorNotes,s=e.example,l=e.passCriteria,c=e.pages,d=e.target;export{t as authorNotes,e as default,o as difficulty,s as example,i as level,n as objectives,c as pages,l as passCriteria,r as subject,d as target,a as title};
